[R0680] year=2026 | venue=2026 56th Annual IEEE International Conference on Dependable Systems and Networks - Supplemental Volume (DSN-S) | type=CONF
TITLE: Securing Blockchain Applications by Transaction Analysis
KEYWORDS: Signal detection; Printing; Security; Smart contracts; Pricing; Blockchains; Conferences; Finance; blockchain; decentralized finance; transaction analysis; attack detection
ABSTRACT: Blockchain applications face billions in annual losses from attacks exploiting smart contract logic vulnerabilities. This doctoral forum paper outlines my thesis: securing blockchain applications through transaction analysis, which extracts high-level semantic features from low-level blockchain data to detect attacks. My completed work, POMABuster, detects Price Oracle Manipulation Attacks via first-principle market manipulation theory with a <1% false positive rate and zero false negative rate. My ongoing work, TxLucent, detects general application-level attacks from function name sequences with 4.49% false negative rate in DeFiHackLab dataset. My future work extends this approach to stealthy attack detection and LLM-driven testing for proactive defense. Collectively, my research contributes a transaction analysis framework bridging specific and general attack detection for blockchain security.

[R0681] year=2026 | venue=SoutheastCon 2026 | type=CONF
TITLE: Instant Segmentation for IoT-Enabled Smart-Grid Networks with a Hybrid Small Language Model Policy Selection
KEYWORDS: Telemetry; Aerospace and electronic systems; Payloads; Feeds; Antennas; Filtering; Central Processing Unit; Filters; Microprocessors; Communication systems; network segmentation; Internet of Things; smart grid; cybersecurity; machine learning; transformers
ABSTRACT: As a key technology that enabled the evolution of traditional grids to next generation smart grids, internet of things has also enabled smart grids to have an expanded attack surface. This paper addresses the problem that Internet of Things are not given immediate network cybersecurity defenses once joining a smart grid which is a major vulnerability. The proposed hybrid machine learning and deep learning-based autonomous decision-making system framework will focus on implementing instant network segmentation configurations deduced from IoT sensor data and network telemetry data. This system is evaluated on a framework that utilizes a testbed that involves network telemetry data relating to cyber-attacks while utilizing a GPT-2–based small language model implemented using the HuggingFace Transformers framework. The hybrid model performed well overall for the logistic regression model and small language model. Results show a near-perfect ROC-AUC performance across evaluated attack classes from the logistic regression models, while the small language model has a perplexity of 1.02 and a TPOT of 31.6 nanoseconds indicating it is very confident and timely in the next token selection. This study supports the concept that instant network segmentation can be achieved for Internet of Thingsenabled smart grids with autonomous network-based defense mechanisms. In this context, ‘instant’ refers to near-real-time policy selection following packet-level inference without human intervention.

[R0682] year=2025 | venue=2025 IEEE 8th Congress on Information Science and Technology (CiSt) | type=CONF
TITLE: AI-generated Papers and Manipulation of Academic Metrics: A Case Study
KEYWORDS: Measurement; Information science; Generative AI; Computer security; ResearchGate; AI-generated Articles; GenAI; Academic Integrity; H-index; Citation Manipulation
ABSTRACT: This paper investigates the presence and impact of questionable, AI-generated academic papers on widely used preprint repositories, with a focus on their role in citation manipulation. Motivated by suspicious patterns observed in publications related to our ongoing research on GenAI-enhanced cybersecurity, we identified clusters of questionable papers and profiles. These papers frequently exhibited minimal technical content, repetitive structure, unverifiable authorship, and mutually reinforcing citation patterns among a recurring set of authors. To assess the feasibility and implications of such practices, we conducted a controlled experiment: generating a fake paper using generative AI (GenAI), embedding citations to suspected questionable publications, and uploading it to one such repository (ResearchGate). Our findings demonstrate that such papers can bypass platform checks, remain publicly accessible, and contribute to inflating citation metrics like the H-index and i10-index. We present a detailed analysis of the mechanisms involved, highlight systemic weaknesses in content moderation, and offer recommendations for improving platform accountability and preserving academic integrity in the age of GenAI.

[R0683] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE World AI IoT Congress (AIIoT) | type=CONF
TITLE: Impact of AI in the Vulnerability management domain - Current research and product trends
KEYWORDS: Artificial intelligence; Modeling; Management; Security; Machine learning; Manuals; Human in the loop; Machining; Printing; Computer security; AI-Powered Vulnerability Management; Predictive Prioritization; Generative AI for Cybersecurity; Adversarial Machine Learning; Model Drift; Human-in- the- Loop (HITL)
ABSTRACT: Artificial Intelligence (AI) is transforming the cybersecurity landscape by enabling organizations to identify, prioritize, and mitigate vulnerabilities with unprecedented speed and accuracy. While traditional vulnerability management has relied heavily on reactive processes, the integration of machine learning, deep learning, and generative AI enables a shift toward proactive and predictive security postures. This paper examines how AI enhances each stage of the vulnerability management lifecycle and evaluates real world implementations from leading cybersecurity vendors such as Tenable, Rapid7, and Darktrace. The paper concludes by discussing the limitations and challenges associated with AI, including model drift, adversarial manipulation, and ethical risks, arguing that human oversight remains crucial for effective and responsible use of AI in vulnerability management.

[R0684] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Computing, Networking and Communications (ICNC) | type=CONF
TITLE: AI-enabled Cybersecurity in the Internet of Things
