ABSTRACT: The Internet of Things (IoT) is transforming industries such as healthcare, smart cities, and industrial systems by enabling interconnected devices to generate and process vast amounts of data. However, the proliferation of IoT introduces significant challenges related to privacy and security due to resource constraints, heterogeneous architectures, and the evolving threat landscape. In recent years, AI has been deeply employed to address IoT Cybersecurity. This paper provides a comprehensive overview of AI and Machine Learning (ML) enabled IoT cybersecurity, covering topics from traditional machine learning, deep learning, federated learning, Large Language Model (LLM) to edge computing in addressing IoT security. This review strives to present a snapshot of the most recent advancement in AI-enabled IoT cybersecurity literacy.

[R0685] year=2025 | venue=IEEE Power and Energy Magazine | type=JOUR
TITLE: Grid Modernization in the Age of Artificial Intelligence and Cloud: Synergies Between Grid Modernization, Artificial Intelligence, and Cloud
KEYWORDS: Cloud computing; Renewable energy sources; Regulation; Market research; Power grids; Artificial intelligence; Resilience; Predictive maintenance; Multi-agent systems; Power system reliability; Electricity supply industry; Energy management; Power distribution planning
ABSTRACT: The electric power grid faces unprecedented challenges as it evolves to meet rising energy demands, integrate renewable sources, and adapt to new technologies. This paper explores how cloud computing and artificial intelligence (AI) are emerging as transformative solutions for grid modernization. Cloud platforms provide the scalable infrastructure and computing power needed to process vast amounts of grid data, while AI enables advanced analytics and decision-making capabilities. Together, these technologies are revolutionizing grid operations across multiple domains. Key applications include intelligent solutions for data center integration, accelerating generator interconnection processes, facilitating distributed energy resource management at scale, enabling data-driven predictive maintenance, enhancing grid resilience and self-healing, optimizing energy generation and distribution, and implementing advanced cybersecurity measures. The article discusses several real-world implementations demonstrating significant operational improvements and cost savings for utilities. It also examines emerging trends like generative AI and multiagent systems that promise to further enhance grid capabilities. While highlighting the immense potential of cloud and AI technologies, the authors emphasize that successful adoption requires a holistic approach encompassing organizational change, workforce development, and regulatory evolution. Looking ahead, the convergence of cloud and AI is poised to create a more intelligent, efficient, and reliable power grid to meet future energy needs. However, realizing this vision will require ongoing collaboration among utilities, technology providers, regulators, and researchers.

[R0686] year=2025 | venue=2025 1st International Conference on Advancement in Futuristic Technologies (ICAFT) | type=CONF
TITLE: GAN-Generated Honeypots for Dynamic Cyber Deception: A Proactive Defense Framework: DeepDeceive
KEYWORDS: Bridges; Adaptive systems; Generative AI; Fingerprint recognition; Network security; Generative adversarial networks; Vectors; Computer security; Cyber Deception; Generative Adversarial Networks; Honeypots; Active Defense; Adversarial AI; Threat Intelligence; Network Security
ABSTRACT: Modern attackers can easily identify and avoid traditional, static honeypots; thus, the efficacy of honeypot systems as an intelligence gathering and threat analysis tool is limited. We introduce DeepDeceive, a novel framework that utilizes Generative Adversarial Networks to create dynamic, adaptive, and highly realistic honeypots. Our approach utilizes a Wasserstein GAN with Gradient Penalty, trained on real network and system data, to generate highly believable "digital ghosts" of synthetic, but realistic, network service banners, system fingerprints, and file structures. These artifacts are then deployed to dynamically reconfigure a honeypot environment in response to adversarial probing. We evaluated DeepDeceive against automated scanning tools and human penetration testers in a controlled testbed. Our results show that attackers spend 68% more time when interacting with DeepDeceive and that honeypot fingerprinting decreases by 45% compared to high-interaction honeypots such as Cowrie. Furthermore, we were able to generate a rich dataset comprising novel attack vectors. This work shows that GANs can significantly enhance cyber-deception by creating a more credible and engaging adversarial playground; this, in turn, enhances threat intelligence and strengthens overall network defense.

[R0687] year=2026 | venue=2026 7th International Conference on Big Data & Artificial Intelligence & Software Engineering (ICBASE) | type=CONF
TITLE: Evaluating Fine-tuned Code Language Models for SSTI Payload Candidate Generation
KEYWORDS: Engines; Modeling; Payloads; Syntactics; Testing; Training; Tuning; Cleaning; Labeling; Generative Pre-trained transformer; SSTI; template injection; language model finetuning; payload candidate generation
ABSTRACT: Server-Side Template Injection (SSTI) is a critical vulnerability class affecting web applications across 9 template engines in Python, Java, PHP, Ruby, and Node.js ecosystems, plus 2 cross-engine payload categories (General and Polyglot). Existing automated SSTI tools rely on static payload dictionaries that lack adaptability and cross-engine coverage. Here we present a systematic study of fine-tuning language models for automated SSTI payload generation. We constructed a curated dataset of 3,949 high-quality, engine-labelled SSTI payloads through a multi-stage cleaning pipeline that removed 57.1% of noisy entries while retaining functional diversity. Fine-tuning GPT-2 (124M parameters) on this dataset yields a model achieving 96.7% syntactic validity and $40 \%$ semantic completeness across a 120-prompt test suite spanning all 9 engines and 2 cross-engine categories, compared to 3.3% syntactic validity for the base model. Engine-aware prompt formatting improves syntactic validity by 83.3 percentage points (from 16.7% to 100%), and data cleaning from V2 to V3 improves syntactic validity by a further 7.5 percentage points (from 89.2% to 96.7%). In live executability tests across eight Docker-based sandbox environments, the model achieves 42.5% execution success (34/80), compared to 37.5% (30/80) for public payload dictionaries. At default generation temperature $(\mathbf{T}=\mathbf{1 . 0}), \mathbf{5 1 . 7 \%}$ of generated payloads are novel relative to the training set (trigram Jaccard < 0.85), indicating recombination rather than memorization of exploitation primitives. This work establishes that small, fine-tuned language models can generate diverse, syntax-valid SSTI payloads and complement existing signature-based tools with machine-learned adaptability. Current limitations include reduced cross-engine (Polyglot) generation capability and the absence of runtime feedback loops for iterative payload refinement.

[R0688] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Intelligent and Sustainable AI Systems (ICOSAAS) | type=CONF
TITLE: Network Access Layer Security in the Age of Artificial Intelligence
KEYWORDS: Artificial intelligence; Modeling; Machine learning; Internet of Things; Machining; Security; Reconnaissance; Media; Signal detection; Protocols; Artificial Intelligence; Machine Learning; Network Access Layer; MAC Spoofing; ARP Poisoning; Wireless Security; Adversarial Machine Learning
ABSTRACT: Artificial Intelligence (AI) is transforming cybersecurity by enhancing both defensive capabilities and offensive cyber operations. While most research focuses on AI-enabled attacks targeting higher layers of the communication stack, less attention has been given to the Network Access Layer, which supports Ethernet, Wi-Fi, MAC addressing, switching infrastructures, and address-resolution protocols. This paper examines how AI amplifies traditional Network Access Layer threats through intelligent reconnaissance, behavioral profiling, device impersonation, protocol exploitation, wireless attacks, radio-frequency intelligence gathering, and large-scale IoT targeting. A taxonomy of AI-driven threats is presented and linked to enabling machine-learning techniques, including reinforcement learning, deep learning, adversarial machine learning, generative AI, federated learning exploitation, and graph analytics. The paper analyzes vulnerabilities affecting hardware, wireless communications, communication protocols, trust models, IoT environments, and AI-based security systems. To address these challenges, a multilayer defense framework is proposed that combines AI-driven intrusion detection, behavioral analytics, explainable AI, Zero Trust principles, secure access-layer controls, and automated response capabilities. The findings demonstrate that AI is fundamentally reshaping the threat landscape at the Network Access Layer and requires adaptive, intelligence-driven security architectures.

